  "passage": {
    "title": "Saving the Honeybee",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Honeybees are small, but they play a very large role in our lives. About one-third of the food we eat depends on pollination, the process by which insects carry pollen from one flower to another, and honeybees do much of this work. Apples, almonds, blueberries and many other crops would be much rarer without them."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Sadly, bee numbers are falling in many parts of the world. In the United States, beekeepers lose around forty per cent of their colonies each year. Scientists have identified several causes. Pesticides, which farmers use to kill harmful insects, also poison bees. Loss of wild flowers, as fields are turned into farmland and car parks, leaves bees with less food. A tiny insect called the varroa mite attacks bees and spreads disease."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Some people say that we do not need to worry because there are many other pollinators, such as butterflies, wild bees and flies. This is partly true, but wild pollinators are declining too, and they cannot replace honeybees on large farms. I believe that this argument underestimates the danger."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "What can be done? Governments can ban the most dangerous pesticides, as the European Union has done with some of them. Farmers can leave strips of wild flowers at the edges of their fields. Ordinary people can help, too. If you have a garden or a balcony, you can plant flowers such as lavender and sunflowers, which provide food for bees from spring to autumn. It is also a good idea to avoid using chemicals on your plants."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Some people worry about bee stings and want to remove nests near their homes. But bees rarely sting unless they feel threatened. If you find a nest, contact a local beekeeper instead of killing the bees; many will collect them for free. Protecting bees is not just about honey. It is about protecting the food on our tables."
      }
    ]
  },
